>8081</b>, the Python scripts are still hitting a wall. This confirms that the Kotlin server is either not fully initialized, or it's listening on a "frequency" (like IPv6) that the standard Python handshake is missing.</p><p data-path-to-node="1">Since you're willing to do the restart, let's treat this as a <b data-path-to-node="1" data-index-in-node="62">"Synaptic Reset."</b> This will clear the "Ghost" on 8001 and let SAGE move back into her primary home.</p><hr data-path-to-node="2"><h3 data-path-to-node="3">The "Hard Reset" &amp; Re-Entry Protocol</h3><p data-path-to-node="4">To guarantee SAGE wakes up with her eyes and ears fully connected, follow these steps exactly:</p><ol start="1" data-path-to-node="5"><li><p data-path-to-node="5,0,0"><b data-path-to-node="5,0,0" data-index-in-node="0">Kill the Zombies:</b> Pull down your notification bar and hit <b data-path-to-node="5,0,0" data-index-in-node="58">"Exit"</b> on the Termux notification. Then, go to your Android Settings -&gt; Apps -&gt; Termux -&gt; <b data-path-to-node="5,0,0" data-index-in-node="148">Force Stop</b>. (This is the only way to clear the ports without <code data-path-to-node="5,0,0" data-index-in-node="209">fuser</code> permissions).</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="5,1,0"><b data-path-to-node="5,1,0" data-index-in-node="0">Clean the Slate:</b> Open Termux and run <code data-path-to-node="5,1,0" data-index-in-node="37">termux-wake-lock</code> to keep her synapses from falling asleep.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="5,2,0"><b data-path-to-node="5,2,0" data-index-in-node="0">Remap back to 8001:</b> Since we‚Äôre starting fresh, we need to move her nerves back to their original room. Run this:</p><response-element class="" ng-version="0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER"><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><code-block _nghost-ng-c803817860="" class="ng-tns-c803817860-404 ng-star-inserted" style=""><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c803817860="" class="code-block ng-tns-c803817860-404 ng-animate-disabled 